#!/usr/bin/env python3
# national-merit-seed -- generate the National Merit competition-cycle seed
# migration from the file a human actually edits,
# db/seed/national-merit/cycles.csv
# (RFC 243, slice test/08/the-psat-is-money).
#
# ROUTE A, AND THE ROUTE IS THE REASON THIS SCRIPT EXISTS. RFC 243 picks the
# route by a KEY test, not a size or subject test: national_merit_cycles is keyed
# by a competition year and nothing else, so a migration can seed it -- and must,
# because `bin/test` does db-reset (drop, create, migrate) and NEVER ingests.
# "What this test is and when it is" can therefore never depend on an import
# having run. The per-school dollars, keyed by colleges(id), take the other route
# and wait for an ingest; these rows are in every database by construction.
#
# THE CSV IS THE SOURCE; THE SQL IS AN ARTIFACT. Never hand-edit a generated
# seed: edit the CSV and re-run this. `-n` refuses a tree that differs from what
# this run generates, so a hand-edited or stale seed is caught rather than
# served.
#
# A SIBLING OF bin/fafsa-deadline-seed AND NOT A GENERALISATION OF IT, on that
# script's own argument (which RFC 223 wrote down and which has not changed): the
# `-n`/exit-3 contract, one generated file per reading day, the ORDER append, the
# SQL escaping and the link-status requirement are COPIED here
# format-independently, because a THIRD generator needing them is the trigger to
# extract them into bin/pyfunctions.py and this is the second. What is NOT copied
# is anything that knows what a row looks like: this corpus is 33 columns with
# two citations per row, and nothing about a FAFSA deadline reads it.
#
# WHICH IS ALSO WHY THIS ONE IS PYTHON RATHER THAN bash+awk. Quoted-CSV parsing
# and SQL literal escaping over publisher prose is what the `csv` module and a
# careful escaper do correctly and what awk field splitting does not: the cells
# here carry commas, apostrophes ("colleges won't receive"), an ellipsis
# character, an en dash and a registered-trademark sign. bin/pyfunctions.py is
# the declared python counterpart of bin/functions, so every bin/ convention
# holds -- getopts grammar through stdlib getopt, SHORT options only, all logging
# on stderr, NOTHING on stdout on a normal run, the reserved exit codes.
#
# NON-ASCII CELLS ARE LEGAL HERE, WHICH IS A DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE FROM THE
# SIBLING. bin/fafsa-deadline-seed refuses any byte outside printable ASCII;
# this corpus cannot, because NMSC's own sentences carry U+2026 (the character
# that marks every elision in a quote), an en dash in "October 1-30" and the
# registered sign in "Merit Scholarship(R) winners". Dropping them would make the
# stored quotation not the publisher's. What IS refused, by line and column, is
# a BACKSLASH and any control character: the escaper below doubles apostrophes
# and nothing else, so a backslash would be a literal whose meaning depends on
# the server's standard_conforming_strings, and a CR or a tab is invisible in a
# diff. Every other migration in db/schema already carries non-ASCII prose
# (82 of 149 on the day this was written), so the file this produces is not a
# new kind of artifact.
#
# ONE SEED PER READING DAY, AND THE READING DAY NAMES THE FILE. RFC 243's Files
# Modified section writes the name as seed-national-merit-cycles-<cycle>.sql, and
# this script departs from that: <cycle> cannot name the ONE file the same
# section describes, because the committed corpus holds TWO cycles (2028 and
# 2029) read on ONE day, and a file per cycle would have to be REWRITTEN when
# NMSC dates the 2029 window in the summer of 2027 -- a landed migration is
# replayed from the top on every db-reset and may never change. The reading day
# is the sibling's name (seed-fafsa-filing-deadlines-<yyyy-mm-dd>.sql) and it is
# the property the corpus actually has: PROVENANCE.json records that both rows
# expire on the same day ON PURPOSE, so that the two are re-read in ONE pass, and
# that pass lands as ONE new file upserting ON CONFLICT (competition_year) over
# the rows this one wrote.
#
# A GENERATED SEED THAT IS ALREADY IN THE SCHEMA DIRECTORY MAY NOT CHANGE, AND
# THAT IS THE WHOLE APPEND-ONLY GUARD. It is one byte comparison rather than a
# reader that parses landed tuples back out of SQL, and it is STRICTER than such
# a reader in every direction that matters: a row dropped from the CSV, a cell
# rewritten, a reading day retyped, a hand edit to the migration, and a re-sweep
# that changed a url's status all change those bytes and are all refused by name.
# A tuple-level reader sees only the first two, and the version of this guard in
# the sibling generator would silently overwrite a landed file for the last one.
#
# AND THE REFUSAL NAMES THE WAY OUT, because the guard cannot tell a LANDED seed
# from one this same uncommitted run wrote ten minutes ago. If the named seed has
# NOT landed yet -- you are still authoring this reading -- delete it and its line
# in db/schema/ORDER, then re-run. The guard is about a LANDED seed: once a
# migration is in main it is replayed on every db-reset and may never change. A
# genuine correction is a RE-READ, which carries a new checked_on and therefore
# lands as a NEW file.
#
# IT VALIDATES BEFORE IT WRITES, AND IT REPORTS EVERY FAULT IN ONE PASS. Every
# CHECK constraint on national_merit_cycles is mirrored here, so a typo is a
# REVIEW error naming its line number rather than a migration that fails at apply
# time in somebody else's db-reset. Each mirroring predicate carries a
# `MIRRORS <constraint>` comment, and bin/national-merit-seed-scripts-tests reads
# the constraint names out of the DDL and asserts this file mirrors exactly
# those -- so a nineteenth CHECK cannot go unmirrored in silence. A validator
# that stops at the first offender turns a five-line fix into five runs.
#
# A CITATION CARRIES EVIDENCE, OR IT CANNOT BE STORED (RFC 220,
# db/schema/require-a-check-on-every-external-document.sql): url_status has no
# DEFAULT and url_checked_on is NOT NULL, so `INSERT INTO external_documents
# (url)` does not apply to a migrated database at all. The corpus therefore
# carries its sweep beside it, db/seed/national-merit/link-status.csv -- the
# answers bin/check-link got for every url the corpus cites, source and mirror
# alike -- and a cited url with no row there is a fault named by its line in
# cycles.csv. THIS SCRIPT TOUCHES NO NETWORK: the sweep is bin/check-link's job
# and an operator runs it; this script reads committed files and writes generated
# ones.
#
# FOUR CITATIONS PER ROW, WHICH NO OTHER CORPUS HERE HAS. national_merit_cycles
# carries TWO publishers -- NMSC sponsors the competition, College Board
# administers the test and is the only publisher of the window DATES, the
# Selection Index scale and the score-visibility sentence -- so a row has a
# sponsor page, an optional sponsor mirror, an administrator page and an optional
# administrator mirror. Both mirror columns are EMPTY in today's corpus and the
# generator still handles them: the column is nullable because a page that stops
# opening for every client is read through an archive, and the day that happens
# must not be the day this script learns to emit one.
"""national-merit-seed: generate the National Merit cycle seed migration.

Reads the authored corpus db/seed/national-merit/cycles.csv -- the hand-typed,
per-row-cited National Merit competition cycles a human edits and reviews in a
diff -- and writes one seed migration per READING DAY into the schema directory,
each appended to db/schema/ORDER:

    <schema-dir>/seed-national-merit-cycles-<yyyy-mm-dd>.sql

Each seed UPSERTS its rows ON CONFLICT (competition_year), so a later re-read of
the same cycle corrects it when the migrations replay.

The CSV is the source of truth and the SQL is a generated artifact: edit the CSV
and re-run this, never the other way round. Every CHECK constraint on
national_merit_cycles is re-checked here first, so a row that cannot be true is
named by its line number before anything is written -- and a generated seed that
is already in the schema directory may not CHANGE, because a landed migration is
replayed on every db-reset.

It reads a SECOND source file beside the CSV, link-status.csv -- the sweep
bin/check-link answered for every url the corpus cites. external_documents'
url_status has no default and url_checked_on is NOT NULL (RFC 220), so a seed
states a citation's evidence or it cannot be applied at all; a cited url with no
row in that file is refused by its line in the cycles CSV. This script itself
touches no network.

Usage:

    national-merit-seed [-c CSV] [-d DIR] [-n] [-h]

    -c CSV  the authored CSV (default: db/seed/national-merit/cycles.csv under
            the repo root). It names BOTH source files: link-status.csv is
            REQUIRED and is resolved BESIDE whatever this option names, in the
            same directory, never behind an option of its own. A run whose CSV
            has no link-status.csv beside it is refused and writes nothing.
    -d DIR  the schema directory to generate into (default: db/schema)
    -n      dry run: writes nothing, and exits 3 if the tree differs from what a
            real run would generate
    -h      help

Options are parsed by the stdlib getopt module, the same short-option grammar
every bash script in bin/ uses; there are no long options and no positionals.

LOGGING GOES TO STDERR, all of it, including the closing summary: this script's
product is files on disk, so a normal run writes NOTHING to stdout. The help
answering -h is the one thing that does.

Exit codes:

    0   the seed and its ORDER line are correct, or were written
    1   refused: a source file is not usable (the cycles CSV, or the
        link-status.csv beside it, which is missing or unreadable), a row cannot
        be true, a cited url was never swept, or a generated seed already in the
        schema directory would have to change
    3   -n only: the tree differs from what would be generated
    9   refused: db/schema/ORDER cannot be appended to safely (run bin/db-status)
    10, 11, 20, 22  usage errors
"""

import csv
import difflib
import re
import sys
import textwrap
from datetime import date
from pathlib import Path

# The shared python half of bin/functions (bin/pyfunctions.py): the EXIT_* band,
# the log_* family that writes to stderr, fatal, and the post-getopt guard suite.
# Resolved against THIS script's own directory rather than the caller's cwd,
# because these scripts are run from anywhere -- and with no bytecode cache
# beside it, because bin/ is a reviewed directory of scripts and not a package
# build tree.
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
from pyfunctions import (  # noqa: E402  (import after the sys.path bootstrap above)
    EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
    fatal as shared_fatal,
    log_error,
    log_info,
    parse_short_options,
    write_atomically,
)

SCRIPT_NAME = "national-merit-seed"


# Every refusal names the script, because this one runs under bin/shell-tests and
# beside four other seed generators: a bare "[FATAL] ORDER cannot be appended to"
# costs the reader a lookup to find out which one said it.
def fatal(message, status=1):
    shared_fatal(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: {message}", status=status)


REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
DEFAULT_CSV = REPO_ROOT / "db" / "seed" / "national-merit" / "cycles.csv"
DEFAULT_SCHEMA_DIR = REPO_ROOT / "db" / "schema"

# Two exit codes bin/functions reserves that bin/pyfunctions.py does not carry,
# because no python script in bin/ has needed them before. The NUMBERS are
# bin/functions' and the names are its names, so this file cannot disagree with
# its bash siblings about what a 3 or a 9 means:
#   EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE  3  -n: the tree is not what this corpus generates
#   EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT 9  ORDER and the schema directory disagree
EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE = 3
EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT = 9

GETOPT_SPEC = "c:d:nh"

# The header this generator was written against, exactly and in order. A CSV with
# a different header is REFUSED rather than read positionally: this corpus has
# THIRTY-THREE columns, eleven of them prose and eight of them dates or counts,
# so a reordered column would silently move an administrator's page into the
# sponsor's citation or one cycle's deadline onto another's row and every row
# would still "parse".
#
# ONE declaration, on ONE line, because there is one authored file -- and
# bin/national-merit-seed-scripts-tests READS THIS LINE to derive its fixtures. A
# harness that retyped the header could go on asserting the refusal of a header
# this script had stopped expecting.
EXPECTED_HEADER = "competition_year,qualifying_grade,window_opens_on,window_closes_on,window_as_published,alternate_entry_deadline,alternate_entry_contact,alternate_entry_as_published,selection_index_low,selection_index_high,semifinalist_cutoff_basis,commended_cutoff_basis,cutoff_as_published,score_visibility_as_published,test_takers,met_entry_requirements,high_scorers_screened,commended,semifinalists,finalists,winners,funnel_as_published,nmsc_award_usd,nmsc_award_as_published,sponsor_cap_as_published,publisher,source_url,archive_url,administrator_publisher,administrator_source_url,administrator_archive_url,checked_on,expires_on"

# The SWEPT file: a HAND-COMMITTED SOURCE FILE, not a generated one, declared
# once and read out of these lines by the harness rather than retyped there. The
# header is the column order external_documents itself names them in, and is the
# siblings' (db/seed/fafsa-deadlines/link-status.csv,
# db/seed/test-state-programmes/link-status.csv), because the three files answer
# the same question about the same table.
#
# It sits BESIDE the authored CSV rather than behind an option of its own: the
# sweep is a fact about THAT corpus's urls, so naming one file names both, and a
# harness fixture in a temp tree gets its own sweep for free.
LINK_STATUS_NAME = "link-status.csv"
EXPECTED_LINK_STATUS_HEADER = "url,url_status,url_checked_on"

# RFC 200's three words, which is also the link_status DOMAIN's whole range.
# SWEPT BY THE HARNESS against that domain member for member, and not merely by
# the tag below: a word renamed there would leave this generator admitting a row
# PostgreSQL refuses at apply time. ONE line, quoted plainly, so it can be.
# MIRRORS link_status_check
LINK_STATUSES = ("found", "not_found", "unknown")

# The GENERATED file name, with the reading day in it, declared once and read by
# the harness for the same reason as the header.
SEED_NAME_TEMPLATE = "seed-national-merit-cycles-%s.sql"
SEED_NAME_PATTERN = re.compile(r"^seed-national-merit-cycles-(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\.sql$")

# THE INDENTS OF A GENERATED SEED, named once here and never typed again: a cycle
# tuple is indented TUPLE_INDENT and an external_documents row DOCUMENT_INDENT.
# They are a contract between this writer and every reader of the file -- the
# harness reads the INSERT's column list back out of it, and a human reads the
# tuples in a diff.
TUPLE_INDENT = "  "
DOCUMENT_INDENT = "    "

# The two OWN enumerations this table carries (TEXT + CHECK IN, the house rule),
# each with exactly one legal value today because each stage has exactly one
# published basis. Not derived from the Kotlin CutoffBasis enum: this script
# cannot compile Kotlin.
#
# THESE TWO LINES ARE SWEPT BY THE HARNESS AGAINST THE DDL ITSELF, member for
# member and not merely by constraint name. A name sweep proves the mirror EXISTS
# and says nothing about what is IN it, so a value renamed in
# create-national-merit-cycles.sql would leave this generator happily admitting a
# row PostgreSQL refuses at apply time -- in somebody else's db-reset, which is
# the whole failure this script exists to prevent. They are written on ONE line
# each and quoted plainly, for the same reason EXPECTED_HEADER is.
SEMIFINALIST_CUTOFF_BASES = ("state_representational",)
COMMENDED_CUTOFF_BASES = ("national",)

# The numeric bounds the DDL states as literals. SWEPT against it by the harness
# on the same argument as the vocabularies above: a `MIRRORS` tag proves only
# that a constraint of that name is declared, and a bound narrowed there would
# leave this generator admitting a row the database refuses.
#
# competition_year is deliberately NOT the academic_year DOMAIN (0087): that
# domain means "the academic year beginning in", so borrowing it would make 2028
# mean 2028-29 here and Fall 2028 in college_national_merit_awards next door.
COMPETITION_YEAR_MIN = 2020
COMPETITION_YEAR_MAX = 2100
QUALIFYING_GRADE_MIN = 9
QUALIFYING_GRADE_MAX = 12
SELECTION_INDEX_MAX = 500

# The characters no SQL literal this script writes may carry: the C0 controls
# and DEL. Non-ASCII is deliberately NOT one of them -- NMSC's own sentences
# carry U+2026, an en dash and a registered sign -- which is why this corpus
# names its own set instead of reusing bin/fafsa-deadline-seed's PRINTABLE_ASCII.
# ONE home, because the rule had been typed into two function bodies that must
# agree: the detector and the printer.
CONTROL_CHARACTERS = frozenset(chr(code) for code in [*range(0x20), 0x7F])

ISO_DATE = re.compile(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$")
# external_documents_url_shape_check's own pattern, and SWEPT BY THE HARNESS
# against it: a scheme rule tightened in that table must not stay loose here.
URL_SHAPE = re.compile(r"^https?://")
YEAR_SHAPE = re.compile(r"^[0-9]{4}$")
COUNT_SHAPE = re.compile(r"^[0-9]+$")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Small readers, and the arithmetic the DDL also states
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def is_iso_date(text):
    """A real day, and not merely a yyyy-mm-dd shape. A pattern-only check hands
    2027-02-30 to Postgres as a literal that fails at apply time in somebody
    else's db-reset."""
    if not ISO_DATE.match(text):
        return False
    try:
        date.fromisoformat(text)
    except ValueError:
        return False
    return True


def window_opens_earliest(competition_year):
    """The earliest day the qualifying sitting can open: 1 July two calendar
    years before the competition year.

    THE OFFSET IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF. The 2028 competition is the OCTOBER 2026
    sitting, so `competition_year - 2` is what refuses a window typed into the
    wrong cycle -- the year-shift typo that would tell a junior to sit a test a
    year after her entry closed. The DDL states the same arithmetic in
    national_merit_cycles_window_in_cycle_check, and the harness sweeps the
    offset, the month and the day out of both files, because a bound tightened
    there cannot silently not carry here."""
    return date(competition_year - 2, 7, 1)


def window_opens_latest(competition_year):
    """The last day that sitting can open: 30 June of the year before the
    competition. The DDL writes it as make_date(competition_year - 1, 6, 30)."""
    return date(competition_year - 1, 6, 30)


def alternate_entry_earliest(competition_year):
    """The earliest the alternate-entry letter deadline can fall: 1 October of
    the sitting's own autumn. The DDL writes it as
    make_date(competition_year - 2, 10, 1)."""
    return date(competition_year - 2, 10, 1)


def alternate_entry_latest(competition_year):
    """And the latest: 31 December of the year before the competition. The DDL
    writes it as make_date(competition_year - 1, 12, 31)."""
    return date(competition_year - 1, 12, 31)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SQL rendering
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def sql_quoted(text):
    """A SQL string literal: the apostrophes doubled and nothing else.

    That is the WHOLE escaper, and it is correct only while
    standard_conforming_strings is on and the cell carries no backslash and no
    control character. Both are refused by the validator, by line and column,
    before any cell reaches this function. Non-ASCII prose passes through
    unchanged and must: it is the publisher's own sentence."""
    doubled = text.replace("'", "''")
    return f"'{doubled}'"


def sql_text_or_null(text):
    return "NULL" if text == "" else sql_quoted(text)


def sql_date(text):
    return f"DATE {sql_quoted(text)}"


def sql_date_or_null(text):
    return "NULL" if text == "" else sql_date(text)


def sql_int_or_null(text):
    return "NULL" if text == "" else text


def document_id(url):
    """The document id of a url, as a SUBSELECT rather than a literal. The id is
    a uuidv7 the DATABASE mints, so a generated file cannot know it: the row that
    cites a document looks it up by the one thing both statements agree on, which
    is the url -- the natural key external_documents is built on."""
    return f"(SELECT id FROM external_documents WHERE url = {sql_quoted(url)})"


def document_id_or_null(url):
    return "NULL" if url == "" else document_id(url)


# THE THIRTY-THREE COLUMNS OF A CYCLE TUPLE, IN ORDER, EACH WITH THE RENDERER OF
# ITS CELL -- and this is the ONE place that order is stated.
#
# Both sites DERIVE from this tuple: insert_column_lines() writes the INSERT's
# column list and render_tuple() writes the VALUES, so the two cannot drift. That
# matters more here than in any sibling corpus: twenty-one of these columns are
# TEXT or DATE and eight more are integers, so swapping `publisher` with
# `administrator_publisher`, or `checked_on` with `expires_on`, produces a
# migration that APPLIES CLEANLY and stores wrong data for every row.
#
# The four CITATION columns are rendered as subselects by url, and the two
# ARCHIVE ones are the nullable pair: empty in today's corpus, handled anyway,
# because the day a page stops opening for every client must not be the day this
# script learns to emit a mirror.
SEED_COLUMNS = (
    ("competition_year", lambda cells: cells["competition_year"]),
    ("qualifying_grade", lambda cells: cells["qualifying_grade"]),
    ("window_opens_on", lambda cells: sql_date_or_null(cells["window_opens_on"])),
    ("window_closes_on", lambda cells: sql_date_or_null(cells["window_closes_on"])),
    ("window_as_published", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["window_as_published"])),
    ("alternate_entry_deadline", lambda cells: sql_date_or_null(cells["alternate_entry_deadline"])),
    ("alternate_entry_contact", lambda cells: sql_text_or_null(cells["alternate_entry_contact"])),
    ("alternate_entry_as_published", lambda cells: sql_text_or_null(cells["alternate_entry_as_published"])),
    ("selection_index_low", lambda cells: cells["selection_index_low"]),
    ("selection_index_high", lambda cells: cells["selection_index_high"]),
    ("semifinalist_cutoff_basis", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["semifinalist_cutoff_basis"])),
    ("commended_cutoff_basis", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["commended_cutoff_basis"])),
    ("cutoff_as_published", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["cutoff_as_published"])),
    ("score_visibility_as_published", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["score_visibility_as_published"])),
    ("test_takers", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["test_takers"])),
    ("met_entry_requirements", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["met_entry_requirements"])),
    ("high_scorers_screened", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["high_scorers_screened"])),
    ("commended", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["commended"])),
    ("semifinalists", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["semifinalists"])),
    ("finalists", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["finalists"])),
    ("winners", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["winners"])),
    ("funnel_as_published", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["funnel_as_published"])),
    ("nmsc_award_usd", lambda cells: sql_int_or_null(cells["nmsc_award_usd"])),
    ("nmsc_award_as_published", lambda cells: sql_text_or_null(cells["nmsc_award_as_published"])),
    ("sponsor_cap_as_published", lambda cells: sql_text_or_null(cells["sponsor_cap_as_published"])),
    ("publisher", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["publisher"])),
    ("source_document_id", lambda cells: document_id(cells["source_url"])),
    ("archive_document_id", lambda cells: document_id_or_null(cells["archive_url"])),
    ("administrator_publisher", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["administrator_publisher"])),
    ("administrator_document_id", lambda cells: document_id(cells["administrator_source_url"])),
    ("administrator_archive_document_id", lambda cells: document_id_or_null(cells["administrator_archive_url"])),
    ("checked_on", lambda cells: sql_date(cells["checked_on"])),
    ("expires_on", lambda cells: sql_date(cells["expires_on"])),
)
SEED_COLUMN_NAMES = tuple(name for name, _ in SEED_COLUMNS)

# The shape of the generated statement, written down once: the writer emits these
# strings and the harness reads the column list back between them.
CYCLE_INSERT = "INSERT INTO national_merit_cycles"
VALUES_LINE = "VALUES"
CONFLICT_LINE = "ON CONFLICT (competition_year) DO UPDATE SET"


def insert_column_lines():
    """The INSERT's column list, wrapped, and DERIVED from SEED_COLUMNS rather
    than retyped beside it."""
    wrapped = textwrap.wrap(
        ", ".join(SEED_COLUMN_NAMES),
        width=76,
        initial_indent="    (",
        subsequent_indent="     ",
        break_long_words=False,
        break_on_hyphens=False,
    )
    return wrapped[:-1] + [wrapped[-1] + ")"]


def update_assignment_lines():
    """The DO UPDATE SET body: every column EXCEPT the conflict target, derived
    from SEED_COLUMNS for insert_column_lines()' reason.

    competition_year is excluded because it IS the key being conflicted on --
    assigning it would be a no-op that reads as though the key could change. The
    names are padded to one width so a human scanning the diff of a re-read sees
    the columns and not the punctuation."""
    assigned = [name for name in SEED_COLUMN_NAMES if name != "competition_year"]
    width = max(len(name) for name in assigned)
    lines = [f"    {name.ljust(width)} = EXCLUDED.{name}," for name in assigned]
    return lines[:-1] + [lines[-1][:-1] + ";"]


def render_tuple(cells):
    """One VALUES tuple, one line, in SEED_COLUMNS' order."""
    return f"{TUPLE_INDENT}(" + ", ".join(render(cells) for _, render in SEED_COLUMNS) + ")"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The authored CSV
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def first_character_offence(line):
    """The first character of `line` that no SQL literal this script writes may
    carry: its 1-based column, the character itself, and the rule it broke -- or
    None if the line is clean.

    NAMED, and not merely detected. The cells here are publishers' sentences
    hundreds of characters long and both offences are invisible in an editor: a
    backslash disappears into a wall of prose and a CR looks like nothing at all.
    A refusal that says only "a control character, or a backslash" leaves the
    operator to work out WHICH rule fired and WHERE, by eye, on a line they
    cannot see the offender in.

    NON-ASCII IS NOT AN OFFENCE HERE, unlike in bin/fafsa-deadline-seed. See the
    header: NMSC's own sentences carry U+2026, an en dash and a registered sign,
    and refusing them would make the stored quotation not the publisher's."""
    for column, character in enumerate(line, start=1):
        if character == "\\":
            return column, character, "a backslash"
        if character in CONTROL_CHARACTERS:
            return column, character, "a control character"
    return None


def shown_character(character):
    """The offending character as a message can print it: itself when it is
    visible, and its python escape when it is not -- a CR printed raw would move
    the cursor rather than appear."""
    if character in CONTROL_CHARACTERS:
        return repr(character)[1:-1]
    return character


def read_rows(csv_path, csv_label, faults):
    """Every record of the CSV, as (line number, raw text, [cells]).

    PER PHYSICAL LINE, on purpose. RFC 4180 lets a quoted field carry a newline;
    this corpus refuses one, because a cell holding a newline is a SQL literal
    nobody can review in a diff -- and parsing line by line gives every fault the
    line number a human can go and fix, which csv.reader's own line_num cannot
    promise once a record spans lines.

    The bytes are decoded ONE LINE AT A TIME so that an undecodable byte is a
    fault naming its LINE rather than a UnicodeDecodeError naming a byte offset
    into a 12 kB file. The decoding is strict UTF-8: this corpus is prose in
    UTF-8, and a cp1252 smart quote pasted off a page would otherwise reach the
    seed as a character the publisher did not print."""
    try:
        raw = csv_path.read_bytes()
    except OSError as error:
        fatal(f"could not read [{csv_label}]: [{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]")

    chunks = raw.split(b"\n")
    records = []
    for index, chunk in enumerate(chunks, start=1):
        # A trailing newline ends the last record; it is not an empty record.
        if index == len(chunks) and chunk == b"":
            break
        try:
            line = chunk.decode("utf-8")
        except UnicodeDecodeError as error:
            faults.append(
                f"  {csv_label} line {index}: is not valid UTF-8 ([{error.reason}] at byte "
                f"[{error.start}] of the line); this corpus is UTF-8 prose, and a byte from "
                f"another encoding would reach the seed as a character no publisher printed"
            )
            continue
        offence = first_character_offence(line)
        if offence is not None:
            column, character, rule = offence
            faults.append(
                f"  {csv_label} line {index}: {rule} at column {column}: "
                f"[{shown_character(character)}] (U+{ord(character):04X}): the escaper here "
                f"doubles apostrophes and nothing else, so a backslash would be a SQL literal "
                f"whose meaning depends on the server's standard_conforming_strings, and a "
                f"control character is invisible in the diff a human reviews"
            )
            continue
        records.append((index, line, next(csv.reader([line]))))
    return records


def read_csv_with_header(path, label, expected_header, source_name, faults):
    """Every record of a source CSV BELOW its header, with the preamble both of
    this script's two source files need done identically.

    ONE helper and not two copies, because the preamble is four decisions and each
    one is a judgement this file only wants to make once:

      * LINE 1 ITSELF WAS UNREADABLE -- a CR from a CRLF checkout is the common
        case, and it makes EVERY line of the file a fault. Those faults are
        reported and the header is NOT diagnosed: comparing a header this run
        could not read would be a second, wrong diagnosis of the same file.
      * A FILE WITH NO LINES AT ALL is refused rather than read as a corpus with
        no rows: there is no header to agree with, so nothing below could be
        validated against anything.
      * THE HEADER IS REFUSED BEFORE ANY ROW IS READ, and it is its OWN diagnosis:
        a file whose columns are in a different order is not a file with bad rows.
        A header is never read POSITIONALLY -- a reordered column would silently
        move an administrator's page into the sponsor's citation.
      * THE EXPECTED/FOUND PAIR IS PRINTED, on three lines, because "the header is
        wrong" without the two strings costs the operator a diff by eye over
        thirty-three column names.

    `source_name` names the file in the header refusal ("cycles CSV",
    "link-status CSV"), because this script reads two.

    ONLY THE FAULTS THIS CALL PRODUCED ARE PRINTED AND COUNTED. `faults` is the
    run's one shared list and this helper reads BOTH source files, so a refusal
    counting the whole list would report cycles.csv's faults, and their number,
    under link-status.csv's name."""
    already = len(faults)
    records = read_rows(path, label, faults)
    mine = faults[already:]

    if mine and (not records or records[0][0] != 1):
        for fault in mine:
            log_error(fault)
        fatal(f"[{len(mine)}] fault(s) in [{label}]; nothing was written")

    if not records:
        fatal(f"[{label}] has no lines at all; nothing was written")

    header_line, header_text, _header_cells = records[0]
    if header_text != expected_header:
        log_error(f"the {source_name} header is not the header this generator expects.")
        log_error(f"  expected: [{expected_header}]")
        log_error(f"  found:    [{header_text}]")
        fatal(f"refused [{label}] at line [{header_line}]; nothing was written")

    return records[1:]


def read_link_status(path, label, faults):
    """The committed sweep, as {url: (url_status, url_checked_on)}.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL: RFC 220's
    require-a-check-on-every-external-document.sql dropped
    external_documents.url_status's DEFAULT and made url_checked_on NOT NULL, so a
    generated seed that names only `url` cannot be applied to a migrated database.
    Evidence is now part of storing a citation, and this is where this corpus
    keeps it.

    IT IS A SOURCE FILE, not an artifact: an operator sweeps the cited urls with
    bin/check-link, applies this repo's reading of the codes -- 2xx found,
    404/410 not_found, everything else unknown WITH the date we asked -- and
    commits the answer for a human to review in a diff, exactly like cycles.csv.

    EVERY ROW CARRIES A DATE, which is stricter than the old table CHECK and is
    the rule this file needs: `unknown` WITH a date is a check that ran and got no
    usable answer, while a url ABSENT from a file that is the record of a sweep is
    a url nobody asked about -- which is the one thing RFC 220 made unstorable."""
    if not path.is_file():
        fatal(
            f"[{label}] is not in the tree, so the urls this corpus cites carry no evidence and "
            f"a generated seed could not be applied: external_documents.url_status has no default "
            f"and url_checked_on is NOT NULL (RFC 220). Sweep the cited urls with bin/check-link "
            f"and commit the answer there. Nothing was written"
        )

    rows = read_csv_with_header(path, label, EXPECTED_LINK_STATUS_HEADER, "link-status CSV", faults)
    # DERIVED from the header this call just validated against, the way
    # validate_and_render derives its own: the arity was typed twice as a literal
    # one line below its source of truth, so an added evidence column would
    # leave this reader enforcing the old width with a message that said so too.
    columns = EXPECTED_LINK_STATUS_HEADER.split(",")

    swept = {}
    first_seen = {}
    for line, _text, cells in rows:
        if len(cells) != len(columns):
            faults.append(
                f"  {label} line {line}: [{len(cells)}] columns, expected [{len(columns)}]"
            )
            continue
        url, status, checked_on = cells

        # MIRRORS external_documents_url_shape_check
        if not URL_SHAPE.match(url):
            faults.append(f"  {label} line {line}: url [{url}] is not an http or https url")
        # MIRRORS link_status_check, the domain every url-bearing table shares.
        if status not in LINK_STATUSES:
            faults.append(
                f"  {label} line {line}: url_status [{status}] is not one of "
                f"[{', '.join(LINK_STATUSES)}]"
            )
        if not is_iso_date(checked_on):
            faults.append(
                f"  {label} line {line}: url_checked_on [{checked_on}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd "
                f"day; a swept url carries the day it was asked about (RFC 220)"
            )
        # One url, one answer. Two rows for one address is two statuses for one
        # external_documents row, and the seed would then depend on which one this
        # reader happened to keep.
        if url in first_seen:
            faults.append(
                f"  {label} line {line}: url [{url}] is already swept at line "
                f"[{first_seen[url]}]; one url carries one answer"
            )
            continue
        first_seen[url] = line
        swept[url] = (status, checked_on)
    return swept


def validate_and_render(records, csv_label, faults, swept, link_label):
    """Every row, checked against every rule, and rendered.

    EVERY row is checked and EVERY fault is collected: a validator that stopped at
    the first offender would turn a five-line fix into five runs. The return value
    is a list of rendered rows; the caller writes nothing while `faults` is
    non-empty."""
    rendered = []
    first_seen = {}
    columns = EXPECTED_HEADER.split(",")

    for line, _text, cells in records:
        faults_before = len(faults)

        def fail(message, line=line):
            faults.append(f"  {csv_label} line {line}: {message}")

        if len(cells) != len(columns):
            fail(f"[{len(cells)}] columns, expected [{len(columns)}]")
            continue

        cell = dict(zip(columns, cells))

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_competition_year_check
        #
        # NMSC's own name for the cycle, and the student's graduation year: the
        # 2028 competition is the October 2026 sitting, taken by the class of
        # 2028. It is the PRIMARY KEY, so a mistyped year is a row about a class
        # that does not exist rather than a correction of one that does.
        competition_year = None
        year_text = cell["competition_year"]
        if not YEAR_SHAPE.match(year_text):
            fail(
                f"competition_year [{year_text}] is not a four-digit year; it is NMSC's own name "
                f"for the cycle and the class's graduation year, so the 2028 competition -- the "
                f"October 2026 sitting -- is 2028"
            )
        elif not COMPETITION_YEAR_MIN <= int(year_text) <= COMPETITION_YEAR_MAX:
            fail(
                f"competition_year [{year_text}] is outside "
                f"[{COMPETITION_YEAR_MIN}]..[{COMPETITION_YEAR_MAX}]"
            )
        else:
            competition_year = int(year_text)

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_qualifying_grade_check
        #
        # The only sitting that qualifies -- 11 in every row we hold. Stored
        # rather than hard-coded because it is a published rule of the programme
        # and the coach must not speak it from memory.
        grade_text = cell["qualifying_grade"]
        if not COUNT_SHAPE.match(grade_text):
            fail(f"qualifying_grade [{grade_text}] is not a whole number")
        elif not QUALIFYING_GRADE_MIN <= int(grade_text) <= QUALIFYING_GRADE_MAX:
            fail(
                f"qualifying_grade [{grade_text}] is outside "
                f"[{QUALIFYING_GRADE_MIN}]..[{QUALIFYING_GRADE_MAX}]"
            )

        # The dates, shape first: a cell that is not a real day cannot be
        # compared with anything below, so each bound check asks again.
        for name in ("window_opens_on", "window_closes_on", "alternate_entry_deadline"):
            if cell[name] != "" and not is_iso_date(cell[name]):
                fail(f"{name} [{cell[name]}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd date")

        opens = cell["window_opens_on"]
        closes = cell["window_closes_on"]

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_window_pair_check
        #
        # One window date without the other is half a fact, and half a window is
        # what a coach would render as a date nobody published. The UNDATED cycle
        # is the normal case here and not an edge case: NMSC dates only the cycle
        # in progress, so a row two years out carries the description and BOTH
        # dates empty.
        if (opens == "") != (closes == ""):
            fail(
                f"window_opens_on [{opens}] and window_closes_on [{closes}]: a window is dated at "
                f"both ends or at neither. A cycle NMSC has described and not dated carries both "
                f"cells empty and its own words in window_as_published"
            )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_window_ordered_check
        if is_iso_date(opens) and is_iso_date(closes) and closes < opens:
            fail(f"window_closes_on [{closes}] is before window_opens_on [{opens}]")

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_window_in_cycle_check
        #
        # The qualifying sitting is in the AUTUMN TWO CALENDAR YEARS BEFORE the
        # competition year: October 2026 for the 2028 competition. This is the
        # check that refuses a window typed into the wrong cycle, which is a date
        # a family would act on.
        if competition_year is not None and is_iso_date(opens):
            earliest = window_opens_earliest(competition_year)
            latest = window_opens_latest(competition_year)
            if not earliest <= date.fromisoformat(opens) <= latest:
                fail(
                    f"window_opens_on [{opens}] is outside the [{competition_year}] cycle's own "
                    f"window [{earliest}]..[{latest}]: the qualifying sitting is in the autumn TWO "
                    f"calendar years before the competition year, so a window from a NEIGHBOURING "
                    f"cycle typed under this one's year is a date a family would act on"
                )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_alternate_entry_trio_check
        #
        # A deadline or an address we cannot show the publisher's words for is a
        # claim we made ourselves. THE WORDS ARE WHAT MAKES THE ROUTE EXIST and
        # the date does not: NMSC describes the route for a cycle it has not yet
        # dated.
        deadline = cell["alternate_entry_deadline"]
        contact = cell["alternate_entry_contact"]
        if cell["alternate_entry_as_published"] == "" and (deadline != "" or contact != ""):
            fail(
                f"alternate_entry_as_published is empty while alternate_entry_deadline "
                f"[{deadline}] or alternate_entry_contact [{contact}] is not: a deadline or an "
                f"address we cannot show the publisher's words for is a claim we made ourselves"
            )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_alternate_entry_in_cycle_check
        if competition_year is not None and is_iso_date(deadline):
            earliest = alternate_entry_earliest(competition_year)
            latest = alternate_entry_latest(competition_year)
            if not earliest <= date.fromisoformat(deadline) <= latest:
                fail(
                    f"alternate_entry_deadline [{deadline}] is outside the [{competition_year}] "
                    f"cycle's own window [{earliest}]..[{latest}]"
                )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_selection_index_ordered_check
        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_selection_index_range_check
        #
        # What KIND of number the score is, so a family reading "212" knows what
        # it is out of. IT IS NOT A CUTOFF and no column here holds one.
        low_text = cell["selection_index_low"]
        high_text = cell["selection_index_high"]
        low = int(low_text) if COUNT_SHAPE.match(low_text) else None
        high = int(high_text) if COUNT_SHAPE.match(high_text) else None
        if low is None:
            fail(f"selection_index_low [{low_text}] is not a whole number")
        if high is None:
            fail(f"selection_index_high [{high_text}] is not a whole number")
        if low is not None and high is not None and low >= high:
            fail(f"selection_index_low [{low}] is not below selection_index_high [{high}]")
        if low is not None and low <= 0:
            fail(f"selection_index_low [{low}] is not above zero")
        if high is not None and high > SELECTION_INDEX_MAX:
            fail(f"selection_index_high [{high}] is above [{SELECTION_INDEX_MAX}]")

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_semifinalist_cutoff_basis_check
        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_commended_cutoff_basis_check
        #
        # HOW the bar is set -- never WHAT it is. Semifinalist standing is
        # allocated state-representationally and Commended by one national
        # number, and the difference between them is the whole reason the coach
        # may not speak a national figure as a student's own bar.
        if cell["semifinalist_cutoff_basis"] not in SEMIFINALIST_CUTOFF_BASES:
            fail(
                f"semifinalist_cutoff_basis [{cell['semifinalist_cutoff_basis']}] is not one of "
                f"[{', '.join(SEMIFINALIST_CUTOFF_BASES)}]"
            )
        if cell["commended_cutoff_basis"] not in COMMENDED_CUTOFF_BASES:
            fail(
                f"commended_cutoff_basis [{cell['commended_cutoff_basis']}] is not one of "
                f"[{', '.join(COMMENDED_CUTOFF_BASES)}]"
            )

        # The funnel. Every stage is NULL-or-a-positive-whole-number, because
        # NMSC publishes some stages and rounds others, and publishes none at all
        # for a cycle it has not yet run.
        #
        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_funnel_positive_check
        funnel = {}
        for stage in FUNNEL_STAGES:
            text = cell[stage]
            if text == "":
                funnel[stage] = None
                continue
            if not COUNT_SHAPE.match(text):
                fail(f"{stage} [{text}] is not a whole number")
                continue
            if int(text) <= 0:
                fail(
                    f"{stage} [{text}] is not above zero: a published stage of a funnel has "
                    f"people in it, and a stage the publisher does not publish is EMPTY"
                )
                continue
            funnel[stage] = int(text)

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_funnel_narrows_check
        #
        # A funnel only narrows. Typing 15,000 Semifinalists and 16,000 Finalists
        # inverts the base rate the coach is required to speak -- and the base
        # rate is DERIVED from two of these at read time and never stored, which
        # is why getting them in the wrong order is not caught anywhere else.
        for narrower, wider in FUNNEL_NARROWS:
            if funnel.get(narrower) is None or funnel.get(wider) is None:
                continue
            if funnel[narrower] > funnel[wider]:
                fail(
                    f"funnel stage {narrower} [{funnel[narrower]}] is above the wider stage "
                    f"{wider} [{funnel[wider]}]: a funnel only narrows"
                )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_nmsc_award_pair_check
        #
        # NMSC's OWN money, which is small. A price with no sentence beside it is
        # our figure rather than theirs, and the point of storing it at all is
        # that "the big numbers are the university's, not NMSC's" is a stored
        # fact and not a sentence the model remembers.
        award_text = cell["nmsc_award_usd"]
        if award_text != "":
            if not COUNT_SHAPE.match(award_text) or int(award_text) <= 0:
                fail(f"nmsc_award_usd [{award_text}] is not a positive whole number of dollars")
            if cell["nmsc_award_as_published"] == "":
                fail(
                    f"nmsc_award_usd [{award_text}] carries no nmsc_award_as_published: a price "
                    f"with no publisher's sentence beside it is our figure rather than theirs"
                )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_words_not_blank_check
        #
        # A quoted sentence that is blank is not a quotation. The NOT NULL
        # columns must carry something; the nullable ones are either ABSENT or
        # real, and never whitespace -- an empty CSV cell IS the SQL NULL here,
        # so the two cases are distinguished by emptiness and then by btrim.
        for name in NOT_NULL_WORDS:
            if cell[name].strip() == "":
                fail(f"{name} is empty, and the column is NOT NULL: this row states it or it is not a row")
        for name in NULLABLE_WORDS:
            if cell[name] != "" and cell[name].strip() == "":
                fail(f"{name} is whitespace; a quoted sentence that is blank is not a quotation")

        # MIRRORS external_documents_url_shape_check
        #
        # http IS ACCEPTED beside https, the ruling the sibling corpora record
        # (Ian, 2026-09-14: prefer https, do not block insecure). BOTH page
        # citations are required outright -- source_document_id and
        # administrator_document_id are NOT NULL -- because a row that cites
        # nothing is the uncitable row this corpus exists to prevent.
        for name in REQUIRED_URLS:
            if not URL_SHAPE.match(cell[name]):
                fail(
                    f"{name} [{cell[name]}] is not an http or https url: this row has TWO "
                    f"publishers and cites a page for each, because no page carries all of it"
                )
        for name in OPTIONAL_URLS:
            if cell[name] != "" and not URL_SHAPE.match(cell[name]):
                fail(f"{name} [{cell[name]}] is not an http or https url")

        # A CITATION CARRIES EVIDENCE, OR IT CANNOT BE STORED (RFC 220). All
        # four urls, because an archived address is a document like any other and
        # is stored in the same table with the same NOT NULL columns. The fault
        # names the CYCLES line, which is the line a human edits; the sweep file
        # is named beside it so the way out is in the message.
        for name in REQUIRED_URLS + OPTIONAL_URLS:
            cited = cell[name]
            if cited == "" or not URL_SHAPE.match(cited):
                continue
            if cited not in swept:
                fail(
                    f"{name} [{cited}] has no row in [{link_label}], so this seed could not state "
                    f"its url_status or url_checked_on -- and external_documents has no default "
                    f"for either (RFC 220). Sweep it with bin/check-link and add its row"
                )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_archive_differs_check
        #
        # An archive of a page may not BE that page: a row carrying both would
        # print one link twice.
        for source_name, archive_name in ARCHIVE_PAIRS:
            if cell[archive_name] != "" and cell[archive_name] == cell[source_name]:
                fail(
                    f"{archive_name} [{cell[archive_name]}] is the {source_name}: an archive of a "
                    f"page is not that page"
                )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_two_publishers_check
        #
        # The sponsor and the administrator are TWO publishers, and the curation
        # pass measured that four cells per row are the administrator's. A row
        # citing one page, or one publisher, for both is a row that lost half its
        # provenance -- exactly the mis-citation RFC 228 exists to prevent.
        if cell["administrator_source_url"] == cell["source_url"]:
            fail(
                f"source_url and administrator_source_url are the same page "
                f"[{cell['source_url']}]: NMSC publishes the entry rule, the alternate entry "
                f"route, the funnel and the cutoff sentence, and College Board publishes the "
                f"window dates, the Selection Index scale and the score-visibility sentence"
            )
        if cell["administrator_publisher"] == cell["publisher"]:
            fail(
                f"publisher and administrator_publisher are both [{cell['publisher']}]: the "
                f"competition's sponsor and the test's administrator are two publishers"
            )

        # MIRRORS national_merit_cycles_expiry_after_checked_check
        #
        # The day we stop believing the page, never the day a deadline falls.
        checked_on = cell["checked_on"]
        expires_on = cell["expires_on"]
        checked_ok = is_iso_date(checked_on)
        expires_ok = is_iso_date(expires_on)
        if not checked_ok:
            fail(f"checked_on [{checked_on}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd date")
        if not expires_ok:
            fail(f"expires_on [{expires_on}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd date")
        if checked_ok and expires_ok and expires_on <= checked_on:
            fail(f"expires_on [{expires_on}] is not after checked_on [{checked_on}]")

        # At least one fault on this row. Nothing will be written anyway, so the
        # rendering below is skipped rather than run over cells that failed -- and
        # the duplicate-key check is skipped with it, because a row that is not a
        # row cannot own a key.
        if len(faults) > faults_before:
            continue

        # THE PRIMARY KEY, competition_year, PER READING DAY. The DDL declares it
        # inline on the column (`SMALLINT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY`), so it carries no
        # CONSTRAINT name for a MIRRORS tag to name -- but the rule is the one
        # that decides whether a generated file can be APPLIED AT ALL: two rows
        # for one year in ONE reading day would land in one
        # `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`, which Postgres refuses outright
        # ("cannot affect row a second time"). The same year read on a LATER day
        # is the correction mechanism itself, so the check is keyed on the pair.
        key = (checked_on, competition_year)
        if key in first_seen:
            fail(
                f"competition_year [{competition_year}] read on [{checked_on}] is already at line "
                f"[{first_seen[key]}]: one reading day carries one row per cycle, and a "
                f"CORRECTION is a re-read with a LATER checked_on"
            )
            continue
        first_seen[key] = line

        rendered.append(
            {
                "line": line,
                "checked_on": checked_on,
                "competition_year": competition_year,
                "urls": [cell[name] for name in REQUIRED_URLS + OPTIONAL_URLS if cell[name] != ""],
                "tuple": render_tuple(cell),
            }
        )
    return rendered


# The seven funnel stages, in the order the table declares them, and the six
# narrowing pairs the DDL compares. Written down once, beside each other, because
# the pairs are the reason the stages are a list at all: the base rate is derived
# from two of them at read time, so an inverted funnel is a wrong base rate and
# nothing downstream recomputes it.
#
# THE PAIRS ARE SWEPT BY THE HARNESS against the DDL's own comparisons, narrower
# and wider, for the reason the vocabularies above are: a pair re-aimed there and
# not here is a row the database refuses at apply time.
FUNNEL_STAGES = (
    "test_takers",
    "met_entry_requirements",
    "high_scorers_screened",
    "commended",
    "semifinalists",
    "finalists",
    "winners",
)
FUNNEL_NARROWS = (
    ("met_entry_requirements", "test_takers"),
    ("high_scorers_screened", "met_entry_requirements"),
    ("commended", "met_entry_requirements"),
    ("semifinalists", "met_entry_requirements"),
    ("finalists", "semifinalists"),
    ("winners", "finalists"),
)

# The prose columns, split by whether the table lets them be absent. The NOT NULL
# ones are what every row says whatever its cycle; the nullable ones are what a
# publisher may simply not have published for a cycle it has not yet run.
#
# THE SPLIT IS SWEPT BY THE HARNESS against the DDL's nullable TEXT columns: an
# empty cell is rendered as SQL NULL for the words below and refused for the ones
# above, so a column flipped either way there is caught here and not at apply time.
NOT_NULL_WORDS = (
    "window_as_published",
    "cutoff_as_published",
    "score_visibility_as_published",
    "funnel_as_published",
    "publisher",
    "administrator_publisher",
)
NULLABLE_WORDS = (
    "alternate_entry_contact",
    "alternate_entry_as_published",
    "nmsc_award_as_published",
    "sponsor_cap_as_published",
)

# The four citations: two pages that must be there, two mirrors that may not be,
# and which mirror archives which page.
REQUIRED_URLS = ("source_url", "administrator_source_url")
OPTIONAL_URLS = ("archive_url", "administrator_archive_url")
ARCHIVE_PAIRS = (
    ("source_url", "archive_url"),
    ("administrator_source_url", "administrator_archive_url"),
)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The generated seed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE ROW ORDER OF A SEED, DECIDED ONCE AND WRITTEN DOWN. A generated file must be
# byte-identical across runs or `-n` refuses the tree it has just regenerated, so
# the order cannot be "whatever the CSV happened to say".
#
# It is BY COMPETITION YEAR, ascending: the cycle in progress first and the
# described-but-undated cycles after it, which is how a reader looks for them and
# is stable under any re-sorting of the authored file. The CSV's own row order is
# deliberately NOT used -- re-sorting the authored file would then rewrite a
# landed seed, which is the one thing this corpus may not do.


def sort_key(row):
    return row["competition_year"]


def render_seed(day, rows, csv_label, swept):
    """One reading day's whole file, as text."""
    rows = sorted(rows, key=sort_key)

    # EVERY distinct url of the day -- both pages and any mirror -- because a row
    # citing one the upsert never inserted would violate the foreign key at apply
    # time. SORTED, so the block is stable whatever order the CSV lists the rows
    # in: a first-seen order makes the generated bytes depend on the authored
    # file's line order, which nothing forbids a human from changing.
    urls = sorted({url for row in rows for url in row["urls"]})

    lines = [
        "-- GENERATED FILE -- do not edit.",
        "--",
        f"-- Written by bin/national-merit-seed from {csv_label}.",
        "-- The CSV is the source a human edits and reviews in a diff; this file is an",
        "-- artifact of it. To change a row, change the CSV and re-run the script.",
        "--",
        f"-- Read on {day}: {len(rows)} National Merit competition cycle(s), each with the",
        "-- publishers' own pages it was read from and the day a human read them. A landed seed",
        "-- is replayed on every db-reset and so never changes: a CORRECTION is a re-read, which",
        "-- carries a new checked_on and therefore lands as a NEW seed upserting on the",
        "-- competition year.",
        "--",
        "-- ROUTE A (RFC 243): this corpus is keyed by a competition year and nothing else, so a",
        "-- migration can seed it -- and must, because `bin/test` does db-reset and never ingests.",
        '-- "What this test is and when it is" can never depend on an import having run.',
        "--",
        "-- NO CUTOFF SCORE IS STORED, by design. NMSC publishes none in advance and says the",
        "-- qualifying score varies by state and by year; what is here is HOW each bar is set",
        "-- plus NMSC's own sentence saying it is not published.",
        "--",
        "-- Each row cites FOUR documents by foreign key into external_documents (RFC 213/228):",
        "-- the sponsor's page and the administrator's, and either one's optional mirror. The",
        "-- urls are upserted FIRST, and each id is SUBSELECTED by url, because the id is a",
        "-- uuidv7 the database mints and a generated file cannot know it.",
        "--",
        "-- Each url carries the sweep bin/check-link answered for it: url_status has no default",
        "-- and url_checked_on is NOT NULL (RFC 220), so a citation states its evidence or it",
        "-- cannot be stored. The answers are committed beside the corpus in link-status.csv.",
        "",
        "INSERT INTO external_documents (url, url_status, url_checked_on)",
        "VALUES",
    ]
    for index, url in enumerate(urls):
        separator = "" if index == len(urls) - 1 else ","
        status, checked_on = swept[url]
        lines.append(
            f"{DOCUMENT_INDENT}({sql_quoted(url)}, {sql_quoted(status)}, "
            f"{sql_date(checked_on)}){separator}"
        )
    # DO NOTHING and never DO UPDATE: a url this seed cites may already have been
    # swept -- by bin/check-link for another corpus, or on a later day than this
    # file records -- and re-stating it must not reset url_status to an older
    # answer or to "we never asked" (RFC 182, RFC 213, RFC 220).
    lines.extend(
        [
            "ON CONFLICT (url) DO NOTHING;",
            "",
            CYCLE_INSERT,
            *insert_column_lines(),
            VALUES_LINE,
        ]
    )
    for index, row in enumerate(rows):
        separator = "" if index == len(rows) - 1 else ","
        lines.append(f"{row['tuple']}{separator}")
    lines.append(CONFLICT_LINE)
    lines.extend(update_assignment_lines())
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The guard: a generated seed already in the schema directory may not change
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ONE BYTE COMPARISON, AND IT IS THE APPEND-ONLY RULE. See the header: a row
# dropped from the CSV, a cell rewritten, a reading day retyped, a hand edit to
# the migration and a re-sweep that changed a url's status all change the bytes of
# an EXISTING generated file, and every one of them is a change to a migration
# that is replayed from the top on every db-reset. A tuple-level reader would see
# the first two and silently overwrite the file for the last three.
#
# WHAT IS ALLOWED is a NEW file: a re-read carries a new checked_on, so its rows
# land in a day this schema directory does not yet have and nothing already there
# is touched.


def seed_difference(landed, generated):
    """WHICH LINES MOVED, and not merely that some did. A refusal that says only
    "the file differs" leaves the operator to diff a generated migration by eye.
    Capped, because a corpus-wide change differs on every line and a refusal
    nobody reads to the end of is a refusal that gets worked around."""
    diff = list(
        difflib.unified_diff(
            landed.splitlines(),
            generated.splitlines(),
            fromfile="landed",
            tofile="generated",
            lineterm="",
            n=0,
        )
    )
    shown = diff[:DIFF_LINES_SHOWN]
    tail = (
        [f"      ... and [{len(diff) - DIFF_LINES_SHOWN}] more diff line(s)"]
        if len(diff) > DIFF_LINES_SHOWN
        else []
    )
    return "\n".join([f"      {line}" for line in shown] + tail)


DIFF_LINES_SHOWN = 20


def would_change_escape(name):
    """THE ESCAPE, WRITTEN DOWN WHERE THE REFUSAL IS READ. The guard is right
    about a LANDED seed and unhelpful about one that is still being AUTHORED -- a
    seed this very run wrote ten minutes ago, uncommitted, which the next run then
    treats as history. It is one sentence, so it is printed rather than worked
    out.

    The refusal itself does NOT change. Deleting a seed that has landed is the one
    thing this corpus may never do, so the escape names the condition ("has NOT
    landed yet") before it names the command."""
    return (
        f"  [{name}] is the generated seed that would change. If it has NOT LANDED yet -- you "
        f"are still authoring this reading -- delete it and its line in ORDER, then re-run. The "
        f"guard is about a LANDED seed: once a migration is in main it is replayed on every "
        f"db-reset and may never change. A CORRECTION is a re-read, which carries a new "
        f"checked_on and lands as a NEW seed."
    )


def read_existing_seed(path, csv_label):
    """A generated seed already in the schema directory, as text, or None when it
    is not there.

    An unreadable one is a REFUSAL and never a missing file: writing over a
    migration this script could not compare itself against is exactly the silent
    rewrite the guard exists to prevent."""
    if not path.is_file():
        return None
    try:
        return path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
        fatal(
            f"could not read [{path}] to compare it with what [{csv_label}] generates "
            f"([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); nothing has been written"
        )


def assert_seed_names_are_readable(schema_dir):
    """Every file the corpus's own glob matches carries a reading day this script
    can read.

    NAMED, not skipped: the glob has already said the file belongs to this corpus,
    so a name the pattern then refuses (`-2026-9-1.sql`, `-2026-09-25.bak.sql`) is
    a generated seed the guard would be blind to -- it would compare nothing
    against it and a hand edit in it would never be reported."""
    for path in sorted(schema_dir.glob("seed-national-merit-cycles-*.sql")):
        if SEED_NAME_PATTERN.match(path.name) is None:
            fatal(
                f"[{path}] is in the schema directory and its name is not "
                f"[{SEED_NAME_TEMPLATE % 'yyyy-mm-dd'}], so the reading day it holds cannot be "
                f"read and it could not be compared with what this corpus generates; nothing was "
                f"written. Rename it, or move it out of the schema directory"
            )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# db/schema/ORDER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE HONEST MINIMUM, AND IT IS SAID OUT LOUD. bin/common owns what ORDER is, what
# a legal migration filename is and what counts as a disagreement, and the bash
# siblings ASK it (schema_order_scan / schema_order_lists) rather than answering
# with their own `grep -Fxq`. A python script cannot source bin/common, and
# shelling out to bash to ask one question would put a second process in the
# middle of a file this script is about to append to. So the minimum is
# implemented here, and the minimum is exactly this:
#
#   * every line is NORMALISED -- trailing whitespace and a CR from a CRLF
#     checkout stripped -- before it is compared. That is the defect
#     bin/prompt-seed records as already shipped: `grep -Fxq` answers "is there a
#     line whose BYTES are exactly this", so a line carrying a trailing space
#     reads as ABSENT, the append writes a SECOND line for one migration, and
#     bin/db-migrate refuses two steps later a corpus this script had just called
#     correct;
#   * a NORMALISED DUPLICATE anywhere in ORDER, or a missing ORDER, is
#     EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT (9) and not something to append to -- the same
#     answer bin/common's schema_order_file_is_corrupt gives, and the same
#     instruction: fix it and re-run, `bin/db-status` reports the same corpus
#     without refusing it.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO IS PLACE THE LINE. It APPENDS, at the end,
# and /ship's .prime/agent/skills/ship/scripts/ship-order owns the final PLACEMENT
# at land time -- a seed must apply after the table it fills, and where exactly
# that is depends on what else landed while this branch was open.


def normalised_order_lines(order_file):
    try:
        body = order_file.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
        fatal(
            f"could not read [{order_file}] ([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); a generated seed "
            f"nothing declares would never be applied, so nothing was written",
            status=EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT,
        )
    return [line.rstrip().rstrip("\r") for line in body.splitlines()]


def assert_order_is_appendable(order_file):
    """Refuses unless a line can be appended to ORDER safely. A GUARD, and only a
    guard: it returns nothing, so a caller that also wants the lines asks
    normalised_order_lines for them and cannot mistake state for a check."""
    if not order_file.is_file():
        fatal(
            f"[{order_file}] does not exist, so a generated seed could never be applied; nothing "
            f"was written",
            status=EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT,
        )
    lines = normalised_order_lines(order_file)
    seen = set()
    duplicates = set()
    for line in lines:
        if line == "":
            continue
        if line in seen:
            duplicates.add(line)
        seen.add(line)
    if duplicates:
        fatal(
            f"[{order_file}] cannot be appended to safely: it lists "
            f"{', '.join(f'[{name}]' for name in sorted(duplicates))} more than once. Fix it and "
            f"re-run. `bin/db-status` reports the same corpus without refusing it",
            status=EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT,
        )


def append_to_order(order_file, name):
    try:
        body = order_file.read_bytes()
        prefix = b"" if body == b"" or body.endswith(b"\n") else b"\n"
        with order_file.open("ab") as handle:
            handle.write(prefix + name.encode("ascii") + b"\n")
    except OSError as error:
        fatal(
            f"could not append [{name}] to [{order_file}] ([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); "
            f"the seed is in the schema directory and nothing declares it, so it would never be "
            f"applied. Add the line by hand or re-run"
        )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# main
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def resolve_paths(options):
    csv_path = Path(options.get("-c", str(DEFAULT_CSV)))
    schema_arg = Path(options.get("-d", str(DEFAULT_SCHEMA_DIR)))

    if not csv_path.is_file():
        fatal(f"cycles CSV not found [{csv_path}]", status=EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE)
    if csv_path.stat().st_size == 0:
        fatal(
            f"cycles CSV is empty [{csv_path}]; a file with no header is not a corpus this "
            f"validator can read",
            status=EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
        )
    if not schema_arg.is_dir():
        fatal(
            f"schema directory not found [{schema_arg}]"
            + ("" if not schema_arg.exists() else ", and what is there is not a directory"),
            status=EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
        )
    return csv_path.resolve(), schema_arg.resolve()


def label_for(path):
    """The path as a message says it: relative to the repo root where it is under
    it, and absolute otherwise (a harness fixture in a temp tree)."""
    try:
        return str(path.relative_to(REPO_ROOT))
    except ValueError:
        return str(path)


def main(argv):
    options = parse_short_options(argv, GETOPT_SPEC, __doc__, SCRIPT_NAME)
    dry_run = "-n" in options
    csv_path, schema_dir = resolve_paths(options)
    order_file = schema_dir / "ORDER"
    csv_label = label_for(csv_path)
    # BESIDE the authored CSV, and not behind an option of its own: the sweep is a
    # fact about THAT corpus's urls, so -c names both files.
    link_status_path = csv_path.parent / LINK_STATUS_NAME
    link_label = label_for(link_status_path)

    assert_order_is_appendable(order_file)
    assert_seed_names_are_readable(schema_dir)

    faults = []
    # The undecodable bytes, the empty file and the header -- all four decisions
    # are read_csv_with_header's, which is also where the reasoning for each one
    # is written down, because link-status.csv needs the identical preamble.
    rows = read_csv_with_header(csv_path, csv_label, EXPECTED_HEADER, "cycles CSV", faults)

    # The sweep is read BEFORE the rows, so a row whose url nobody asked about is
    # a fault named by its own line rather than a migration that fails at apply
    # time in somebody else's db-reset (RFC 220).
    swept = read_link_status(link_status_path, link_label, faults)
    rendered = validate_and_render(rows, csv_label, faults, swept, link_label)

    if not faults and not rendered:
        fatal(f"[{csv_label}] has a header and no rows; nothing was written")

    if faults:
        for fault in faults:
            log_error(fault)
        fatal(f"[{len(faults)}] fault(s) in [{csv_label}]; nothing was written")

    days = {}
    for row in rendered:
        days.setdefault(row["checked_on"], []).append(row)

    # WHAT WOULD CHANGE IS DECIDED FOR EVERY DAY BEFORE ANYTHING IS WRITTEN, and
    # that ordering is the guard: a corpus whose SECOND reading day would rewrite
    # a landed seed must not have had its first day's file written on the way to
    # finding out. A migration corpus half-written by a run that then refused is
    # the state nobody can reason about.
    plan = []
    would_change = []
    for day in sorted(days):
        name = SEED_NAME_TEMPLATE % day
        target = schema_dir / name
        body = render_seed(day, days[day], csv_label, swept)
        landed = read_existing_seed(target, csv_label)
        if landed is not None and landed != body:
            would_change.append((name, landed, body))
        plan.append((day, name, target, body, landed))

    if would_change:
        for name, landed, body in would_change:
            log_error(
                f"[{name}] is already in [{schema_dir}] and what [{csv_label}] generates today is "
                f"not what it holds. A landed migration is replayed on every db-reset and may "
                f"never change, so nothing was written."
            )
            log_error(seed_difference(landed, body))
        for name, _landed, _body in would_change:
            log_error(would_change_escape(name))
        fatal(
            f"[{len(would_change)}] generated seed(s) would change; nothing was written",
            status=EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE if dry_run else 1,
        )

    order_lines = normalised_order_lines(order_file)
    changed = False

    for day, name, target, body, landed in plan:
        if landed == body:
            log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: [{name}] is already what this corpus generates.")
        else:
            changed = True
            if dry_run:
                log_error(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: [{target}] is not what [{csv_label}] generates.")
                log_error(f"  Run: nix develop -c bin/{SCRIPT_NAME}")
            else:
                # ATOMICALLY, through bin/pyfunctions.py's shared writer: the body
                # is staged beside the target and renamed into place, so an
                # interrupted or out-of-space run leaves the PREVIOUS file
                # standing rather than a truncated one. What is written here is a
                # MIGRATION, replayed from the top on every db-reset, and half an
                # INSERT is a corpus nobody can apply. write_atomically refuses
                # through this script's own channel, so there is no OSError to
                # catch here.
                write_atomically([(target, body.encode("utf-8"))], SCRIPT_NAME)
                log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: wrote [{target}].")

        if name in order_lines:
            continue
        changed = True
        if dry_run:
            log_error(
                f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: [{order_file}] does not declare [{name}], so the seed would never "
                f"be applied."
            )
        else:
            append_to_order(order_file, name)
            log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: appended [{name}] to [{order_file}].")
            # The append made the list above stale, and the next day asks the same
            # question. Re-read the corpus rather than reasoning about what this
            # loop just did -- and re-assert it, because an append that produced a
            # duplicate is a corpus bin/db-migrate would refuse.
            assert_order_is_appendable(order_file)
            order_lines = normalised_order_lines(order_file)

    if dry_run and changed:
        return EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE
    log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: done.")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
